+ * <pre>
+ *   phase   shader teeth   java teeth        shader aura            java aura
+ *   -----   ------------   --------------    --------------------   -------------------
+ *   4       white          (1.00,0.45,0.85)  (0.55,0.80,1.00)       (0.25,0.50,1.00)
+ *   5.0     white          (1.00,0.35,0.90)  (1.00,1.00,1.00) PURE  (0.30,0.55,1.00)
+ *   5.2     white          (0.85,0.40,1.00)  (0.50,0.78,1.00)       (0.22,0.48,1.00)
+ *   6       white          (0.70,0.35,1.00)  (0.22,0.42,1.00)       (0.18,0.42,1.00)
+ *   7       white          (0.90,0.30,0.95)  (0.36,1.00,0.28) GREEN (0.20,0.50,1.00)
+ *   8       white          (0.80,0.40,1.00)  (0.35,0.58,1.00)       (0.25,0.55,1.00)
+ * </pre>
+ *
+ * So the mouths the shader draws and the mouths the model's emissive layers draw
+ * were two different mouths from phase 4 onward -- which is exactly the symptom the
+ * header claimed to have fixed ("the old table made the teeth themselves cyan / blue
+ * / green, which is why the mouths never matched the reference frames"). A later
+ * "V2 revamp" moved the Java tables without moving the shader, and because {@code
+ * check_phase_uniform} only ever inspected the shaders, nothing caught it.
+ *
+ * <p>Java now takes its values FROM {@code mcsm_aura_color()} / {@code
+ * mcsm_teeth_color()} verbatim. Three independent sources agree the teeth are white
+ * or cyan-white -- this file's own header, {@code refs/GROUND_TRUTH.md} B.1/E/G.3
+ * ("chunky bright white/cyan rectangular blocks", "soft cyan-white (~200,235,245)").
+ * The pink/purple table was the outlier.
+ *
+ * <h2>Intensity -- left alone, and why</h2>
+ *
+ * GROUND_TRUTH.md G.3 says: <i>"Teeth brightness: soft cyan-white (~200,235,245),
+ * intensity ~1.15 -- not the old pure-white 2.4 blast."</i> {@code
+ * ci/check_phase_uniform.py} pins a 4.0x floor ("the glow is guaranteed from phase 4
+ * up, at 4.0x") and the entity shader carries {@code MCSM_MOUTH_GAIN = 4.0},
+ * citing a later brief. <b>Those two instructions contradict each other and nothing
+ * in the tree says which supersedes which</b>, so #478 changes only the colour
+ * columns -- which are provably wrong, as the table above shows -- and leaves the
+ * intensity exactly where it found it. Brightness is a judgement call for the user,
+ * not something to resolve silently. If G.3 wins, the change is one number in the
+ * force block plus the gate's own expectation.
+ *
+ * <h2>BUILD #478 -- the config is borrowed, not taken</h2>
+ *
+ * {@link #tick()} writes nine fields of {@code DabyWSClientConfig}, which is the
+ * player's persistent client config. It used to write them unconditionally, every
+ * tick, with no snapshot and no restore, so:
+ *
+ * <ul>
+ *   <li>{@code glowStrength = Math.max(glowStrength, 4.5F)} was a one-way ratchet.
+ *       Walk near one storm and the player's bloom setting was permanently 4.5,
+ *       for every world, forever, with nothing to put it back.</li>
+ *   <li>the storm's eye/beam/teeth colours outlived the storm. Nothing restored
+ *       them on the way out, so the last values a storm wrote stayed in the player's
+ *       config file and became their "normal" colours.</li>
+ * </ul>
+ *
+ * The fields are now snapshotted once when the modules first take over at phase 4
+ * and restored when the storm falls back below it, so the config is borrowed for the
+ * duration rather than spent.
  */
